#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064722 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{-2/3}$ and that $F^{′}(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=3x^{1/3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\sqrt[3]{x}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064723 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{3/5}}$ and that $F^{′}(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 x^{\\frac{2}{5}}}{2}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064853 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x^{4}}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule:\n$$\\int_1^2x^{-4}\\,dx=\\left[-\\frac1{3x^3}\\right]_1^2=-\\frac1{24}+\\frac13=\\frac7{24}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064854 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6+1}}{-6+1}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5}}{(-5)}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5{x}^{5}}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{(1/2)}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-{2}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-32) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064855 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}}dx\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "On this nonnegative interval, the integrand is $x^{3/5}$. Thus\n$$\\int_0^1\\sqrt[5]{x^3}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac58x^{8/5}\\right]_0^1=\\frac58.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064877 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]+[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0]+[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064878 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-{\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{2}{9}[{e}^{3(1/3)}-{e}^{3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{e}{3}-0]-\\frac{2}{9}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{9}-\\frac{2e}{9}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068623 :question/problem "Find the slope of each of the lines below.\n\n![](images/e9/e901704deb02efe957129512148c97e42b45b4fdd1d0e3af451562cca2ed1181.png)" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the vertical line is undefined. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(4,0)$ and $(4,1),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-0}{4-4}=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the horizontal line is zero. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,3)$ and $(1,3),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{3-3}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068669 :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48114216e210a4e4dfc353871bbfd16bd2beb480f5831c62189db9299e09f517.png)" :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, we let $(x_1,y_1) = (-2,0)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (0,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-0}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068670 :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $(x_1,y_1) = (1,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tm &= \\dfrac {y_2 - y_1} {x_2 - x_1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {2 - 5} {4 - 1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {-3}{3} \\\\\n\t\t    &= -1\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068671 :question/problem "Given the following graph, what can we say about the sign of the slope?\n\n![](images/ff/ffa6dd91f72bc66c943e88aa764a64811391a00af162b40320ce3d90f96b16a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($\\searrow$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069083 :question/problem "What is the slope of a line that passes through the points $(4, 5)$ and $(2, 3)$?" :question/worked-solution "Use the slope formula: $$m=\\frac{3-5}{2-4}=\\frac{-2}{-2}=1.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069195 :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddbdc756cdd3fb6109cd1ffaf06df2cca4d33c01578860192632c8efe778a425.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y = 4$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x = 2$. Find its slope." :question/worked-solution "The intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{0-4}{2-0}=-2.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069343 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8dcd33d9f2ae449eb066d248127b01b0cefa63aec697da2a5f0b8c05959e4ca.png)\n\nCalculate the slope of the line shown above." :question/worked-solution "The plotted line is horizontal: its $y$-coordinate stays constant as $x$ changes. Its rise is zero, so its slope is $0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069344 :question/problem "![](images/10/10263a4490f8d69ad33b0fdb26a5cfac6617c6a0cfdf2727268947551013ec26.png)\n\nDetermine the slope of the line shown above." :question/worked-solution "The plotted line is vertical. Any two distinct points on it have zero horizontal change, so the slope would require division by zero. The slope is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069348 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbc939fa11b93eba400fffa92442762cda6b42edcaafa8cfd099ca4d9e8de7cd.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the line that passes through the points plotted in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points are $(1,0)$ and $(3,6)$. Thus $$m=\\frac{6-0}{3-1}=3.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069451 :question/problem "![](images/25/25f8b258817ad92a5aafc929cfbfb5efe5289b1fd12c2c0e1b49b3d5970a5786.png)\n\nThe straight line shown above intersects the $y$-axis when $y = 2$ and intersects the $x$-axis when $x =-4$. Find its slope." :question/worked-solution "The intercepts are $(-4,0)$ and $(0,2)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{2-0}{0-(-4)}=\\frac12.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069452 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5bb5ca3f96989cfdd00b430cf6c215c906ce7ef926eb4ecd3c020d2422ae9dcf.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "The line falls as we move from left to right. Thus its rise is negative for a positive run, and its slope is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069511 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f4e58fe2d8f9235737eac4efa80e3b75812373b87676623ae9227355240cf33.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the slope of the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "The line rises as we move from left to right. Thus its rise and run have the same sign, and its slope is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=4 \\\\ x+2y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\\\n\t3y &=12\\\\\n\ty &=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\\\n\t   &=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6 &=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\\qquad\\:\\:\\:\n\\begin{cases}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{cases}.$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073997 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3p + q &=-9 \\\\ 5p + 4q &=32\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$." :question/worked-solution "The first equation gives $q=3p-9$. Substitute into the second equation: $$5p+4(3p-9)=32\\implies17p=68\\implies p=4.$$ Hence $q=3(4)-9=3$. Substitution into both original equations gives $-9$ and $32$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073998 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ \\phantom{-}6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The second equation gives $b=-a$. Substitute into the first equation: $$-7a+2(-a)=18\\implies-9a=18\\implies a=-2.$$ Then $b=2$. Indeed, $-7(-2)+2(2)=18$ and $6(-2)+6(2)=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074030 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + 5y &=13 \\\\ y &=x + 5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "Substitute $y=x+5$ into the first equation: $$x+5(x+5)=13\\implies6x=-12\\implies x=-2.$$ Then $y=-2+5=3$. Both original equations hold, so the ordered solution is $(-2,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074047 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5q + 55 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 3q + 3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From $5q+55=0$, we obtain $q=-11$. Substituting into the second equation gives $2p-33+3=0$, so $p=15$. Therefore $p-q=15-(-11)=26$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074172 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a + 2b &=4 \\\\ - 4a + b &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The second equation gives $b=2+4a$. Substitute into the first equation: $3a+2(2+4a)=4$, so $11a=0$. Thus $a=0$, $b=2$, and $a-b=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p-q?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p &=15 \\\\ p+2q &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =15 \\\\ p & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=5$ into $p+2q=9,$ then we can find the value of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2q & =9 \\\\ (5)+2q & =9 \\\\ 2q & =4 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=5,\\,q=2.$ So,\n\n$$\np-q=5-2=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083545 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d40bd1a54762b360dc590f014d1df9731168f8620df07ee8f0294bd078fc8da5.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{4} + t, y = t^{2}, - ∞ < t < ∞.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t =-1$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "At $t=-1$, the point is $(0,1)$, while $x'(t)=4t^3+1=-3$ and $y'(t)=2t=-2$. The tangent slope is $2/3$, so the normal slope is $-3/2$. The normal is $y=1-3x/2$; setting $y=0$ gives $x=2/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{2em}{y}^{'}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083611 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}}{4{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-4{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=5,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}(5-1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A vertical tangent (and horizontal normal) requires x′(t)=0 and y′(t)≠0. Here $x′(t)=- 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y′(t)=10 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. Within the given half-open interval the solutions are $\\left\\{0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083613 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t - 1, y = t^{3} - 2, - ∞ < t < ∞.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "Since $dx/dt=1$ and $dy/dt=3t^2$, we have $dy/dx=3t^2$. A horizontal tangent requires $dy/dx=0$, which gives $t=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083615 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"}]}
